 "sentences": [
  "[concerned] She asked, \"Are we, though?\" He said, \"We're fine.\" The account said three hundred and ten dollars, eight days before payday.",
  "So, like every month, she pulls money back out of savings to get to payday. Same fight, every month.",
  "[thoughtful] And no, \"just save first\" won't fix it. They tried. The money came straight back out, because nobody knew what was safe to spend after it left.",
  "Here's what fixes it: one number.",
  "They take home eighty-four hundred a month. Fifty-six hundred of it is bills.",
  "Six hundred went to savings on payday. Everything after that was a guess.",
  "Savings still goes first: six hundred dollars, the day the paycheck lands.",
  "But now the other twenty-two hundred becomes one number for each day: about seventy-three dollars.",
  "Saturday's big grocery run is a hundred and ninety. That's a hundred and seventeen over.",
  "So DivvyUpp spreads it across the next six days. Each one drops to about fifty-three.",
  "[warm] Spending stays inside the twenty-two hundred, so checking never runs dry, and nothing gets pulled back. Eight days before payday, she doesn't have to ask. The six hundred is still saved.",
  "Send this to whoever says \"we're fine.\"",
  "Save first. Then spend what's safe. Try it at Divvyup dot com."
 ],
